<section class="section" id="quickstart">
  <h2>Quickstart</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">
    Three commands with the docker CLI, or one paste in Portainer or TrueNAS.
  </p>
  <div class="prose">
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>Make a directory, download the compose file, and write a <code>.env</code> beside it with the address you will browse to and three fresh secrets. Then start it:</p>
        <pre class="code"><code>mkdir engrafo &amp;&amp; cd engrafo
curl -fsSLO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/cubicecho/engrafo/main/docker-compose.quickstart.yml

# The three secrets it refuses to start without, plus the address you reach it at.
printf 'ENGRAFO_HOST=%s\nBETTER_AUTH_SECRET=%s\nPOSTGRES_PASSWORD=%s\nRUSTFS_PASSWORD=%s\n' \
  "$(hostname -f)" "$(openssl rand -hex 32)" "$(openssl rand -hex 24)" "$(openssl rand -hex 24)" &gt; .env

docker compose -f docker-compose.quickstart.yml up -d</code></pre>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <strong>Check <code>ENGRAFO_HOST</code>.</strong> <code>hostname -f</code>
          is a guess. It has to be the hostname or LAN IP you will actually open
          Engrafo at, with no scheme: <code>truenas.local</code>,
          <code>192.168.1.50</code>. Edit <code>.env</code> if the guess is wrong.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <strong>Open <code>http://ENGRAFO_HOST:3004</code>.</strong> Migrations
          run at boot and the job creates both buckets (one for your files, one
          for the text extracted from them), so there is no setup step.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h3>On Portainer or TrueNAS SCALE</h3>
    <p>
      Paste the contents of
      <a href="{{ site.repo }}/blob/main/docker-compose.quickstart.yml" rel="noopener"><code>docker-compose.quickstart.yml</code></a>
      instead of downloading it: Portainer's <em>Stacks → Add stack → Web
      editor</em>, or TrueNAS's <em>Apps → Discover → Custom App → Install via
      YAML</em> (Dockge works the same way). Put <code>ENGRAFO_HOST</code>,
      <code>BETTER_AUTH_SECRET</code>, <code>POSTGRES_PASSWORD</code> and
      <code>RUSTFS_PASSWORD</code> in the stack's environment-variables box, and
      <code>TZ</code> (e.g. <code>America/Chicago</code>) if you want it. Make
      each secret with <code>openssl rand -hex 32</code> for
      <code>BETTER_AUTH_SECRET</code> and <code>openssl rand -hex 24</code> for
      the two passwords. The stack refuses to start until the three secrets are
      set, which is deliberate. The file's header comments are the same
      instructions as this page.
    </p>

    <h3>Why <code>ENGRAFO_HOST</code> is not <code>localhost</code></h3>
    <p>
      Your browser uploads files straight to RustFS, over a link Engrafo signs,
      and the signature covers the hostname and the port. If
      <code>ENGRAFO_HOST</code> says <code>localhost</code> while you browse from
      another machine, uploads fail with a signature error rather than one that
      names the real problem. That is also why RustFS's port has to be reachable
      from your browser, not only from Engrafo.
    </p>

    <h3>If the ports are taken</h3>
    <p>
      If something on the machine already owns 3004, 9000 or 9001 (9000 is
      popular), set <code>ENGRAFO_PORT</code>, <code>RUSTFS_PORT</code> or
      <code>RUSTFS_CONSOLE_PORT</code> in <code>.env</code> rather than editing
      the file. The addresses Engrafo signs its upload links with are built from
      these, so changing a port only in the <code>ports:</code> list breaks
      uploads.
    </p>

    <h3>Keep the <code>.env</code></h3>
    <p>
      <code>BETTER_AUTH_SECRET</code> signs sessions, so changing it signs
      everyone out. Your documents and their text live in the
      <code>engrafo_rustfs</code> volume and their metadata in
      <code>engrafo_pgdata</code>. Both survive <code>docker compose down</code>.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="first-sign-in">
  <h2>First sign-in</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Sign in with any email address. Engrafo sends no mail: the sign-in link is
      written to the container log, and that is how you get it.
    </p>
    <pre class="code"><code>docker logs -f engrafo          # or the Logs tab in Portainer</code></pre>
    <p>
      The first account ever created is the admin. The admin is the only one who
      sees instance settings, such as <strong>Settings → Sign-in</strong>.
      Registration is open to anyone who can reach the page, so keep it on a
      network you trust until you have read
      <a href="#security">Before you expose it</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Fetching a link from the log every time gets tedious on a home network.
      <code>SECURE_LOCAL_NET=true</code> skips the link entirely, so entering an
      address signs you in. That makes the address the entire credential, so use
      it only on a LAN you control. Or set up <a href="#sso">single sign-on</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="configuration">
  <h2>Configuration essentials</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">
    The variables the quickstart file reads from <code>.env</code> (or
    Portainer's environment box). Change one, then run <code>up -d</code> again.
  </p>
  <div class="prose">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Variable</th><th>Default</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>ENGRAFO_HOST</code></td><td>required</td><td>The hostname or LAN IP you open Engrafo at, no scheme. Sign-in links and signed upload links are built from it.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>BETTER_AUTH_SECRET</code></td><td>required</td><td>Signs sessions and hashes magic-link tokens. At least 32 characters: <code>openssl rand -hex 32</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>POSTGRES_PASSWORD</code></td><td>required</td><td>The database password: <code>openssl rand -hex 24</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>RUSTFS_PASSWORD</code></td><td>required</td><td>The bucket's secret key, and the RustFS console's root password: <code>openssl rand -hex 24</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>TZ</code></td><td><code>UTC</code></td><td>Time zone for all four containers, e.g. <code>America/Chicago</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>ENGRAFO_PORT</code>, <code>RUSTFS_PORT</code>, <code>RUSTFS_CONSOLE_PORT</code></td><td><code>3004</code>, <code>9000</code>, <code>9001</code></td><td>The published ports. Change them here, never only in the file.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>SECURE_LOCAL_NET</code></td><td><code>false</code></td><td><code>true</code> signs you in from an address alone, with no link. A LAN you control only.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>SESSION_STORE</code></td><td><code>memory</code></td><td><code>memory</code> signs everyone out when the container restarts; <code>database</code> keeps sessions.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>TRUST_PROXY</code></td><td><code>false</code></td><td>Behind a reverse proxy, the number of proxies in front (usually <code>1</code>).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>TRASH_RETENTION_DAYS</code></td><td><code>30</code></td><td>How long a deleted document waits in the Trash, restorable, before it and its files are deleted for good.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>WATCH_OWNER</code></td><td>unset</td><td>Turns on the <a href="#watched-folder">watched folder</a>, filing into this account.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>WATCH_STABLE_SECONDS</code></td><td><code>10</code></td><td>How long a file must stop changing before the watched folder takes it.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>OIDC_ISSUER</code>, <code>OIDC_CLIENT_ID</code>, <code>OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET</code>, <code>OIDC_NAME</code></td><td>unset, <code>SSO</code></td><td>Pins an OpenID Connect provider. See <a href="#sso">Single sign-on</a>.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Some settings are written into the file's <code>environment:</code> block
      rather than read from <code>.env</code>: OCR is on with
      <code>OCR_LANGUAGES=eng</code> and <code>OCR_CONCURRENCY=1</code> (OCR is
      CPU-bound, so raise it only with cores to spare), and
      <code>DATE_ORDER=DMY</code>, which reads <code>03/04/2025</code> day first.
      A US archive wants <code>MDY</code>. Edit those lines under the
      <code>engrafo</code> service to change them. Anything else from the full
      list, such as <code>MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES</code> (100 MiB by default), has to be
      added there too.
    </p>
    <p>
      The full list, with every default, is in the README's
      <a href="{{ site.repo }}#configuration" rel="noopener">Configuration</a>
      section.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="watched-folder">
  <h2>A scanner's watched folder</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">
    Engrafo can file whatever a scanner saves to a network share, then delete it
    from the share.
  </p>
  <div class="prose">
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          Sign in once with the account the files should go to. Then set
          <code>WATCH_OWNER</code> to its email address, e.g.
          <code>WATCH_OWNER=you@example.com</code>. Until that account exists,
          the log says so and nothing is filed.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Under the <code>engrafo</code> service, uncomment the two
          <code>volumes</code> lines and put the share's path on this machine
          before <code>:/consume</code>:
        </p>
        <pre class="code"><code>    volumes:
      - /mnt/tank/scans:/consume</code></pre>
      </li>
      <li><p>Redeploy (<code>docker compose -f docker-compose.quickstart.yml up -d</code>).</p></li>
    </ol>
    <p>What to expect:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Subfolders become folders.</strong> <code>Taxes/2026/receipt.pdf</code> is filed in <code>Taxes</code> → <code>2026</code>, made if missing. A name already taken in a folder is numbered, <code>scan (2)</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>A file is taken once it stops changing</strong> for <code>WATCH_STABLE_SECONDS</code> (10). Raise it for a slow scanner. The share is polled, because file-change events do not cross a network share.</li>
      <li><strong>It is deleted only once it is stored.</strong> Engrafo needs permission to delete files in the share; if it cannot, each file is filed once and then stays, and the log says so.</li>
      <li><strong>The same bytes are not filed twice.</strong> A file already in the owner's archive is removed without adding a copy.</li>
      <li><strong>A file Engrafo cannot take</strong> (a type it does not accept, too big, empty) stays where it is, and the log says why.</li>
      <li><strong>Skipped silently:</strong> dotfiles, <code>~$</code> and <code>*.tmp</code>/<code>*.part</code> files, and NAS housekeeping folders such as <code>@eaDir</code> and <code>#recycle</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      More detail in the README's
      <a href="{{ site.repo }}#the-watched-folder" rel="noopener">The watched folder</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="sso">
  <h2>Single sign-on (OIDC)</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">
    Sign people in through any OpenID Connect provider, such as heautou, next to
    magic links.
  </p>
  <div class="prose">
    <h3>Pairing from the app (the easy way)</h3>
    <p>For a provider that supports Dynamic Client Registration. No variables needed.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>In the provider, make a setup URL for Engrafo. It looks like <code>https://&lt;heautou&gt;/oidc#setup=…&amp;name=heautou</code> and works once, for a short while.</li>
      <li>Paste it into Engrafo. On a new instance, before anyone has an account, that is the login page's <strong>Set up sign-in</strong> tab; after that it is <strong>Settings → Sign-in</strong>, which only the admin sees.</li>
      <li>The login page shows "Sign in with heautou" straight away, with no restart.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Disconnecting in Settings removes the button and deletes Engrafo's client at
      the provider. Accounts stay, and can still sign in with a link.
    </p>

    <h3>From the environment</h3>
    <p>For a provider without registration, or to pin the provider so it cannot be changed from the app:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Register a client at the provider whose redirect URI is exactly <code>http://ENGRAFO_HOST:3004/api/auth/callback/oidc</code>.</li>
      <li>Set <code>OIDC_ISSUER</code>, <code>OIDC_CLIENT_ID</code>, <code>OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET</code> (leave it unset for a public client) and, for the button, <code>OIDC_NAME</code>.</li>
      <li>Redeploy. The login page then shows "Sign in with &lt;OIDC_NAME&gt;", and the server logs the redirect URI it expects.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Set <code>OIDC_ISSUER</code> and <code>OIDC_CLIENT_ID</code> together:
      one without the other stops the server at boot. An issuer set in the
      environment wins over a pairing, and Settings then shows it read-only.
      Someone signing in this way gets the account with the email address the
      provider vouches for, or a new one.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="mcp">
  <h2>Connecting an AI agent over MCP</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">
    Engrafo serves one MCP endpoint, <code>/mcp</code> (Streamable HTTP), next to
    the app. An agent sees only the folders you share, and cannot change anything.
  </p>
  <div class="prose">
    <ol>
      <li>Open a folder's ⋯ menu and choose <strong>Share with MCP</strong>. The folders inside it are shared too. Everything else, including documents at the top level, stays out of reach.</li>
      <li>
        <p>In <strong>Settings → MCP</strong>, make an API key. It is shown once, with the command to paste:</p>
        <pre class="code"><code>claude mcp add --transport http engrafo http://ENGRAFO_HOST:3004/mcp \
  --header "Authorization: Bearer engr_…"</code></pre>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Any other client takes the same URL and header (<code>x-api-key</code> works
      too). The tools are <code>engrafo_folders</code>, <code>engrafo_search</code>,
      <code>engrafo_list</code> and <code>engrafo_read</code>. Turning a folder off
      takes effect on the agent's next call, and revoking a key refuses it from
      then on. A key works as its owner, and needs to be there even with
      <code>SECURE_LOCAL_NET</code>, so treat it like a password.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="backups">
  <h2>Backups</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <h3>Your archive: export and import</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Settings → Backup</strong> exports your whole archive as one
      <code>.tar</code>: every original you uploaded, the searchable PDF and
      extracted text where there are any, and a <code>metadata.json</code> with
      titles, folder paths, dates, receipt fields and checksums. It streams
      straight from the bucket to your browser. The tree inside reads like your
      folders (<code>originals/Taxes/2026/invoice.pdf</code>), so it is useful
      without Engrafo too.
    </p>
    <p>
      The same tab imports one back, into this instance or a new one (a
      <code>.tar.gz</code> too, up to 5 GiB by default). A document already in
      your archive is skipped, so importing the same export twice adds nothing.
      Dates and receipts you corrected by hand are kept, and every document is
      processed again like a new upload, so a large import takes a while to
      settle. Folder sharing with MCP is not restored.
    </p>
    <h3>The whole instance</h3>
    <p>
      An export is per account. To back up the instance itself (accounts, API
      keys, sign-in settings), back up the <code>engrafo_pgdata</code> and
      <code>engrafo_rustfs</code> volumes together, as a database dump plus a
      bucket sync. One without the other is not a working archive.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="security">
  <h2>Before you expose it to the internet</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">
    Registration is open: any address that completes a sign-in gets an account.
    That is right for an instance only you can reach, and wrong on the public
    internet. Before giving Engrafo a domain:
  </p>
  <div class="prose">
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Put it behind something.</strong> A reverse proxy with TLS and, if the instance is yours alone, an allowlist, VPN or auth in front of it. Set <code>TRUST_PROXY</code> to the number of proxies in front, or every visitor counts as the proxy and shares one sign-in rate limit.</li>
      <li><strong>The bucket is reachable too.</strong> Browsers upload and download directly, so RustFS's port is published. Terminate TLS in front of it and set <code>S3_PUBLIC_ENDPOINT</code> to that address.</li>
      <li><strong>Drop the RustFS console port</strong> (<code>9001</code>) from the file if you do not want it exposed. It takes the root password.</li>
      <li><strong>Never set <code>SECURE_LOCAL_NET=true</code></strong> (or the older <code>AUTH_MAGIC_LINK=false</code>) on a reachable instance. Either one lets anyone who can load the login page sign in as anyone.</li>
      <li><strong>Never set <code>EXPOSE_MAGIC_LINK=true</code></strong> on a reachable instance. It hands the sign-in token to whoever asked for it.</li>
      <li><strong>Treat MCP keys like passwords.</strong> Anyone with one reads the folders you shared.</li>
      <li><strong>Set a real <code>BETTER_AUTH_SECRET</code></strong> and keep it. Changing it signs everyone out; leaking it lets anyone mint a session.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="upgrading">
  <h2>Upgrading</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <pre class="code"><code>docker compose -f docker-compose.quickstart.yml pull &amp;&amp; docker compose -f docker-compose.quickstart.yml up -d</code></pre>
    <p>
      Migrations run at boot, and documents that finished before the upgrade get
      any new processing step in the background. On Portainer or TrueNAS,
      redeploy the stack with the latest image. With the default
      <code>SESSION_STORE=memory</code>, the restart signs everyone out.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>From a stack older than search:</strong> search needs Postgres with
      pgvector, so the <code>postgres</code> service's image is now
      <code>pgvector/pgvector:pg17</code> instead of
      <code>postgres:17-alpine</code>. Paste the new file again, or change that one
      line, before you pull. The data volume is kept as it is. On its first boot
      Engrafo rebuilds its database indexes and then indexes every document it
      already has, in the background; search answers from what is done so far.
      Without the new image, the <code>engrafo</code> container stops at boot and
      its log says so.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="minio">
  <h2>Coming from MinIO</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Earlier versions of the quickstart file ran MinIO. RustFS cannot read a
      MinIO data directory, so the new stack starts on an empty
      <code>engrafo_rustfs</code> volume and leaves <code>engrafo_minio</code>
      untouched beside it. Your files are not lost, but Engrafo lists documents
      it cannot open until they are copied across. The README's
      <a href="{{ site.repo }}#coming-from-a-minio-install" rel="noopener">Coming from a MinIO install</a>
      has the commands: rename the <code>MINIO_</code> variables to
      <code>RUSTFS_</code>, start RustFS alone, run the old MinIO from its volume
      and <code>mc mirror</code> both buckets across. Remove the old volume only
      once you have opened a few documents from the new stack.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p class="readme-link">
    Full documentation lives in the project README:
